    "case_id": "mxh055",
    "case_summary": "A 33-year-old male construction worker is brought to the emergency department with acute confusion after being found unconscious at his worksite. His medical history includes seizure disorder and schizophrenia, for which he takes haloperidol. He underwent an appendectomy two months ago. On examination, his vital signs show: temperature 105°F (40.6°C), blood pressure 120/84 mmHg, heart rate 150/min, respiratory rate 19/min, and oxygen saturation 99% on room air. The patient is confused and non-communicative, with sweat-soaked clothing. While he shows no clear focal neurologic deficits, he lacks purposeful movement of his extremities. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Anticholinergic toxicity\nB. Exertional heat stroke\nC. Malignant hyperthermia\nD. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome\nE. Central nervous system infection\nF. Drug-induced hyperthermia\nG. Septic shock\nH. Nonexertional heat stroke\nI. Thyroid storm\nJ. Status epilepticus\n",
